architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "sheni_07"
  title: A boolean that may be unknown is a three-valued enum, and `bool` stays two-valued
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-29"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    ADR sheni_03 gave `bool` no fallback, so a boolean field cannot be
    optional. The reasoning was that `false` is an answer rather than an
    absence, and a flag reading false because nobody set it is
    indistinguishable from a flag deliberately turned off. That record also
    admitted this is where the rule costs something visible, since an
    optional flag is the most commonly wanted optional field there is.

    ADR sheni_06 then answered the same problem for dates, and the answer
    generalised: where a value may genuinely be unknown, the fix is a type
    whose value space includes the unknown, not a field that might be
    missing. `soft_date` has a fully unspecified date as a member of the
    type, so an optional `soft_date` field needs no sentinel.

    The boolean case is the same case, and it is older than this project.
    "Do you have a passport?" has three answers in every survey ever run --
    yes, no, and nobody asked. Databases have modelled this as a nullable
    boolean for forty years, which is the same conflation of "false" with
    "unrecorded" that ADR sheni_03 refused, moved into the storage layer.
  decision: |
    A new type, `soft_bool`, with three values: `false`, `unknown`, and
    `true`.

    This record first placed it in group 3 as an enum, since it is a named set
    of variants over one backing primitive, which is what an enum is here.
    ADR sheni_09 moved it to group 1. Nothing this record decides about the
    type changed -- the same three values, the same order, the same operations
    -- but `soft_bool` is no longer bespoke. It is the boolean member of a
    soft twin for every primitive that can spell its own unknown, and one
    mechanism for softness in group 1 beats a family with an exception beside
    it. Read the group placement as amended; read everything else as written.

    The order is `false` at 0, `unknown` at 1, and `true` at 2.
    That ordering is not arbitrary and not alphabetical. It is Kleene's
    strong three-valued logic, the same three-valued logic SQL uses, in which
    the truth ordering runs false below unknown below true. With the values
    ranked in that order the operations are arithmetic on the ranks:

        and   is the minimum of the two ranks
        or    is the maximum of the two ranks
        not   is 2 minus the rank

    Those three operations ship with the type, per ADR sheni_03's rule that a
    curated type carries the behaviour that makes it worth curating. A shape
    without them sends every consumer to rediscover the same truth tables,
    and the tables are exactly where people get three-valued logic wrong.

    `soft_bool`'s fallback is `unknown`, and it is the second type to have
    one after `soft_date`. This refines ADR sheni_03's statement that an enum
    has no fallback because no variant is more absent than another. That
    holds for every enum in the library -- no month is more absent than
    another, no country is -- and it holds because those enums enumerate
    answers. `soft_bool` enumerates two answers and one absence, and the
    absence is a member by construction rather than a variant promoted to
    stand in for one. An enum therefore has a fallback exactly when one
    variant means "not known" by design, which is a property of how the enum
    was drawn and not something a schema author may assert later.

    `bool` is unchanged and stays two-valued, with no fallback and no
    optional fields. Widening it would put the third state in front of every
    consumer that has a settled yes or no, and a field declared `bool` should
    promise the question was answered. The two types together are the whole
    point: an unset `soft_bool` reads `unknown`, which the consumer must
    handle, rather than reading `false`, which the consumer would not notice.

    What does not ship is SQL's comparison semantics. Three-valued logic is
    adopted for the operations on this type only. Nothing here makes equality
    on any other type return `unknown`, and nothing propagates unknown
    through comparisons elsewhere. Importing the logic is the value; importing
    the surrounding behaviour is where three-valued systems become famous for
    surprising people.

    Errors continue the complex band once it is allocated. Reading follows
    ADR sheni_03's rule for enums unchanged: a scalar holding a variant name,
    with the backing a separate thing for storage and wire formats.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - The most commonly wanted optional field becomes expressible without weakening `bool`, which is what ADR sheni_03's rule was protecting.
      - An unset value reads `unknown` and forces the consumer to decide, rather than reading `false` and being missed, which is the failure ADR sheni_03 named.
      - The backing order makes `and`, `or` and `not` the minimum, maximum and complement of the backings, so the implementation is hard to get wrong and easy to check.
      - Shipping the operations puts the truth tables in one place, and the truth tables are the part of three-valued logic people reliably get wrong.
      - The rule for when an enum has a fallback is now stated, so the next enum that wants one is a question with an answer rather than a precedent argument.
    negative:
      - A second boolean is a question every schema author now has to answer, and some will take `soft_bool` because it is more forgiving rather than because the value is genuinely unknown.
      - Three-valued logic is genuinely harder to reason about than two-valued logic, and code handling a `soft_bool` has a third branch that is easy to write and easy to write wrongly.
      - The refinement to the enum fallback rule is a judgement about how an enum was drawn, and judgements are harder to apply consistently than the flat rule they replace.
      - Adopting Kleene's operations while refusing SQL's comparison semantics is a distinction that will have to be explained more than once.
      - The variant names `false` and `true` read as booleans in a document, so a reader has to know the declared type to know which of the two types they are looking at.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Give `bool` a fallback of `false` and drop the whole question
      reason: Rejected in ADR sheni_03 and rejected again here. A flag nobody set reading as off is silent, and the point of this record is that the honest answer exists and can be named.
    -
      name: Widen `bool` to three states rather than adding a type
      reason: Every consumer of every boolean field would then handle a third state, including the ones whose field is a settled yes or no. A field declared `bool` should promise the question was answered.
    -
      name: A simple type over a constrained text form, in group 2
      reason: It is a named set of variants over a backing primitive, which is the definition of an enum in this crate. Group 2 is for an interpretation validated against a standard, and there is no text form to validate here beyond the three names.
    -
      name: Order the variants `unknown`, `false`, `true`, so the fallback is zero
      reason: It puts the fallback at rank 0, which is tidy and costs the arithmetic. Kleene's truth ordering is what makes `and` a minimum and `or` a maximum, and a tidy zero is not worth hand-written truth tables.
    -
      name: Adopt SQL's three-valued semantics wholesale, including comparisons
      reason: The operations on this type are well defined and useful. Unknown propagating through equality on every other type is a different and much larger decision, and the one that gives three-valued logic its reputation.
    -
      name: Let a schema author nominate any enum variant as the fallback
      reason: It would let `month` default to January, which is a sentinel with a friendly name. Whether an enum contains an absence is a property of how it was drawn, decided once by whoever drew it.
